= Ontology — Technical =

== Specification perspective ==
A formal ontology can be modeled as vocabulary plus axioms:

<pre>
O = (V, A)
</pre>

where V contains symbols for classes, relations/properties, individuals or datatypes and A constrains their interpretation.

Exact structure depends on the ontology language.

== Ontological commitment ==
A representation makes ontological commitments by choosing what kinds of entities and distinctions it allows or requires.

Davis, Shrobe and Szolovits treat this as one major role of knowledge representation: a representation focuses attention on one view of the world and suppresses others.

== Formal interpretation ==
Under model-theoretic semantics, ontology axioms define conditions on interpretations.

For ontology O, a model I satisfies O if:

<pre>
I ⊨ O
</pre>

Logical consequences are statements true in every model of O.

This makes inference depend on explicit semantic commitments.

== Top-level / domain ontology ==
A top-level ontology provides very general distinctions such as:
* object / process;
* continuant / occurrent;
* quality;
* relation;
* time / space.

Domain ontologies specialize these concepts for a specific field.

Alignment is useful only when the upper-level commitments are compatible with the domain's intended meaning.

== Expressivity ==
Ontology languages vary in expressivity.

Greater expressivity permits richer axioms but can make reasoning more computationally expensive or undecidable.

Description logics were developed partly to control this trade-off.

== Identity ==
Identity axioms are especially strong.

Declaring two names identical means they denote one individual under the formal semantics.

In OWL, <code>owl:sameAs</code> is not a fuzzy match, synonym or approximate mapping.

== Ontology quality ==
Technical evaluation can include:
* consistency;
* satisfiability;
* competency-question coverage;
* clarity of definitions;
* reuse;
* modularity;
* provenance;
* stable identifiers;
* change management;
* reasoning performance.

Logical consistency alone does not guarantee that the ontology models the intended domain well.

